inline.s:
The mighty earth is a speck of dust,
The vast ocean a single drop.
Through the boundless reaches of space
All things give way and never stop.

The man of understanding takes the great view,
Looking up and down through a thousand ages.
All that has passed lies before his eyes;
Worthies and fools he can count on his fingers.

Beauty and ugliness are not worth naming;
Right and wrong — how can they be compared?
Confucius prized the knowing of Heaven’s decree;
Laozi valued the forgetting of self.

But our Great Hero, the World-Honored One,
Rises beyond birth and death altogether.
The world is a bubble on the water;
Body and mind are but dregs of dust.

Illusion and transformation are only thus;
Honor and disgrace — what grief or joy?
Let the inverted mind take empty flowers as real;
This is all I see of it.

A little boat carries the bright moon,
Racing along with the rushing stream.
The sail’s shadow seems to shift and turn;
The moonlight neither comes nor goes.

Mind and surroundings are at root still and void;
Where then are death and birth?
Wherever one dwells is lasting joy;
All beyond is dust and dirt.

Its transformations have no beginning or end;
Its breathing makes the clouds and rain.
Its rich bounty moistens all living things;
Its pouring torrent soaks the land below.

In a flash it fills the ninefold heavens;
In a moment it covers all the world.
How unlike the worm within the sand,
That rots in stench along with other things.

A long roar issues and the mountain is empty;
A mournful wind shakes all the trees.
Rustling, cold comes the autumn rain;
Wailing, the night ghosts weep.

Its rank and bloody smell it flaunts in vain;
Its hide and fur are fit to be worn.
How much better the peace of the burrowing mole,
That drinks from the river and is content with a full belly.

You came toward Caoxi,
Straight along the Caoxi road.
By the stream I suddenly met you,
And at first sight it was as of old.

One smile, and eye and mind open;
Host and guest forget the forms of courtesy.
We sit knee to knee deep into the night,
Unfolding clearly our inmost hearts.

Your high resolve is bright as snow and ice;
Your pure words ring like gold and jade.
You gaze down and the six directions are empty;
You set out upon a thousand-mile road.

At the year’s end you go on a far journey;
The principle is dark — there is no going or staying.
I take your hand and see you off;
Alone on the bridge I linger.

Mount Wutai in the three hottest weeks;
Jiangnan in the twelfth-month trees.
A lone trace is a cloud in the sky;
My remaining life is dew on the grass.

Cold and heat have no beginning or end;
North and south, I let them go or stay.
Wherever I may be there is coolness enough;
What else is there here to long for?

In youth I was steeped in the will to wander far;
Long have I admired the master of Donglin.
He opened the White Lotus Society in the green hills,
And all the high worthies were gathered there.

They treasured the hours and set the lotus water-clock,
Purifying themselves and bowing at the six periods.
Their pure mindfulness cut off all dusty thought;
The Pure Land was their appointed home.

Their high wind has stirred a thousand years;
I lift my head and knit long thoughts.
Their shining countenances seem before my eyes;
In dreams I follow after them.

Only in declining years did I climb up here,
And touching the scene my sorrow grows.
Wild woods shade the fallen walls;
Rank grass grows thick again.

I linger where the three men smiled;
Moss and lichen show their bloom.
In the shadow hall the host of worthies stands,
And I seem to see their fragrant rule.

The old steps are locked in cold mist;
White lotuses open in the muddy pool.
Fragrant Valley sends out clear echoes;
The pipes of earth are blown by the wind of heaven.

Hills and mounds may shift and change;
The ultimate Way knows no alteration.
The masters had a vow unfinished —
Why should they be slow to come again?

If the grove were opened as of old,
Their lofty traces could still be followed.
The mountain spirit has long protected it;
The sacred power is still here.

I am ready to await my end;
From now on I take leave of the muddy world.

Since I parted from you, thousands of miles;
My longing for you runs on like flowing water.
The water races east and does not return;
My heart is like a ring with no end.

I lift my head and gaze at the long sky;
The long sky is endless and far.
I wave my hand and invite the bright moon;
The bright moon has its time of coming.

Though light and shadow may look at one another,
They can be watched but never reached.
If only your face were like the full moon,
That one look might open my heart.

Hou Sheng’s poverty shames the guests of the Yi Gate;
Who comes to his house with bridle in hand?
He alone has thoughts of painting that enter the spirit;
His elegance is fit to be called utter folly.

His one room hangs empty, clear as snow and ice;
Mist and cloud rise at times from his pores.
Deer and elk run to and fro in the empty air;
Mountain spirits start in fright night after night.

The days are long; he shuts his door and sweeps his yard;
Fine meat is not enough, mist and sunset glow fill him.
He soaks his ink and takes it for ambrosia;
The people of the time wonder at his looks.

For many years now, through the sixty-year cycle,
He has spent half of it in constant hunger.
A foot of cloth, a peck of grain, traded for good wine;
Clear spring and white stone make men mock him.

Sometimes he stands alone at the street corner;
Seeing people, before he speaks he is ashamed.
Everyone says a poor man’s bones are soft as mud;
Who would believe his heart is hard as iron?

The three rivers and five lakes roll in vast waves;
A thousand cliffs and ten thousand ravines contend in strange shapes.
Idly he spreads the silk, lightly wields the brush,
And gathers them all at once on his brow’s tip.

He goes into the mountains seeking out stumps and snags;
Beneath the pines, at one meeting, they call out in joy.
Drinking tea, he regrets a thousand cups are too few;
Asking after Dharma, he has never had half a word.

Old Hou has a big belly and a long tongue;
He swallows and spits the light of the moon for ten thousand miles.
The stars scatter down and no one gathers them;
You, sir, are fit to be my ode-bag.

Let me keep you for now, lying in the mountains,
With white clouds for a canopy and the blue sky for a tent.
When thirsty, drink the clear stream, chew the purple mushrooms;
Let the sea dry and the stones rot — who cares?

In former days I roamed the land of wondrous adornment,
And saw the udumbara flower, white as snow.
Beneath the flowers stood rows of great beings,
Their countenances shining with golden light.

This flower does not open at any common time;
Once in a thousand years it forms its bud.
When a true man is about to appear, the flower blooms first —
It comes alone for the great matter of conditions.

The great matter of conditions is not of one kind;
A hundred thousand million bodies all save the world.
The three vehicles and eight assemblies are answered as thought arises;
The dharma-realms of the ten directions come as the mind wills.

Sometimes he manifests the body of a great heavenly general,
His awesome bearing on every side like a god of heaven.
He sweeps across ten thousand miles, all but unmatched;
The comet is wholly swept away and the smoke and dust cleared.

His transformations have no beginning or end, most rare;
They are like the udumbara opening in a smile.
Playing the flute beneath the moon, the phoenix comes;
Brandishing the spear on horseback, the lion roars.

Once opened, it becomes a portent for the world;
The man and the flower-spirit are not two.
The man imitates Jetavana’s mind of spreading the ground;
The flower becomes the garden’s work of merit.

The general does not depart from Chan in the use of arms;
His small hermitage is built beside Jetavana.
Facing the flowers, his mind enters the land of no-birth;
Shutting his door, his body dwells in the heaven of Ultimate Bliss.

The killing intent is wholly the wisdom that subdues demons;
Color and fragrance wondrously reveal the meaning of the Patriarch’s coming.
Seeing form, smelling scent, the dharma-realm is empty;
On the spot, in battle, he enters samadhi.

The flower’s master moves in the rich creative force;
His literary brilliance runs free and surpasses ancient and modern.
His springtime decrees send forth thunder;
Wind and cloud change their form and drive dragons and tigers.

The garden is vast, and the flowers are unharmed;
The hermitage is just one fathom square.
Ancient and modern, heaven and earth are empty within a mustard seed;
The world, its mountains and rivers, is an image in a mirror.

The udumbara in the mirror is doubly full of spirit;
The general too is a man within the mirror.
Ten thousand miles hang in the void like a face before him;
How can the eye hear as well as the ear sees truly?

I too am a historian beneath the Jetavana flowers,
Watering them always with the water of dhyana.
The creeping weeds of the five aggregates were long ago cut down;
The four elements, this phantom body, have no support.

I cast it out to the burning wilds like a proclamation;
Cauldron of boiling water and furnace of coals meet no resistance.
Suddenly I saw the old master among the flowers;
Send word: do not forget the road by which you came.

Withered-Wood Hut, Withered-Wood Hut,
Before Elephant Ridge, the lion’s lair.
Its gnarled branches twist beyond all climbing;
Frosted leaves spread through the clouds in tangled strands.

Looked at from afar, it seems a stump not worth the taking;
Approached, it is withered and dry as true investigation.
If one set this in Old Man Xue’s hall,
How many Manjusris, before and behind?

I am already old and decayed, my sea of knowing dried;
Let me sit with you in the Withered-Wood Hut.

The burning house blazes and the dream is not yet woken;
Within the dust there is one realm of coolness.
One sees only the raging flames mounting the sky,
While the man of the Way keeps a single thought like a frozen well.

The market’s din roars like ten thousand galloping horses;
Day and night it never rests — for what?
His ear-root is stilled and the mind does not arise;
All of it, he sees, is falsehood in the air.

Wife and children face him like phantom men;
He looks back and sees himself as a shape in a mirror.
All day they meet and do not know one another;
Where do his two eyes find room for a speck of dust?

Sometimes his spirit roams the realm of the lotus treasury,
And one sweep of the brush releases the block within his breast. (Langqu is skilled at painting.)

The marshes of Chu are not the burning southern border;
Walking and chanting, I am ashamed to be the only sober one.
Miasmic smoke blackens a thousand peaks;
Last year’s grass is green in all four seasons.

The hurricane strikes and the autumn waves rage;
Men shrink before the fierce ghost’s power.
From of old all has been drifting traces;
And today I drift still more like duckweed.

Who can escape the burning house?
Coolness can be sought of oneself.
The sky hangs low, near the sun;
The trees are old and do not know autumn.

How still the mind before the sea’s moon;
The empty clouds seem about to float.
Yet I pity the guest who dwells nowhere —
Today he lodges again in the burning isle.

Of old they spoke of the Leiyang road;
Now I pass west of Dianbai.
Ten thousand mountains close in their mist;
One staff is lost in the miasmic smoke.

On the last stretch I follow the tumbling weeds;
This remnant life trusts to the horse’s hooves.
How can I bear it, deep in the trees,
Where the partridge cries on every side?

On the long road where I walk,
A lone cloud is all I can lean on.
Whoever has a home is still a guest;
Whoever has no ties is a monk.

Poisonous mist fumes till the mind is drunk;
The burning wind steams through the bones.
I turn and think of the places I once roamed —
They seem like treading on layer on layer of ice.

Wandering was always my own affair;
To shoulder my umbrella is well within my power.
My heart hangs like the moon for ten thousand miles;
My shoulder bears a single staff of rattan.

In eating I am troubled by the barbarous tongue;
In sitting in Chan I delight in common monks.
Subduing demons — in vain one speaks of the sword;
Today, at last, I take the foremost way.

Though I have left the world, I still march on campaign;
Who pities the hardship of the road?
A long spear serves me for a staff;
My close-cropped hair will not bear a cap.

I drink the evening mist of the three isles;
The burning heat plagues a hundred barbarous tribes.
Where I turn my head in the southern sky,
The setting sun there is Chang’an.

Heaven above covers all things;
Why should it shut out the distant wilds?
Twisting and turning, my life is short;
The roads of the world are long to ride.

I only know my mind is like snow;
Suddenly I feel my temples are like frost.
Anywhere on earth a man may bury his bones;
How can I forget the sovereign’s grace?

Once I dwelt in the realm of coolness;
Now I climb the heaven of burning affliction.
The air of warmth and cold is different;
North and south, the land lies atilt.

Ten thousand miles share the same bright moon;
A thousand mountains are parted by dark mist.
Though the frontier goose may carry a letter,
It will not pass beyond Wild-Goose Peak.

From my childhood I followed the pure karma;
In declining years I laid aside the black robe.
Can it be that the sovereign’s grace was thin?
It must be that the world’s way runs contrary.

My luggage of mist and sunset glow is little;
Snow and ice are seldom before my eyes.
Do not ask about the road ahead;
Home hills are found wherever I am.

At dawn I rise and divine the weather;
The river of stars parts the dawn light.
The tide swallows the red phoenix sun;
The mountains spit out the poisonous dragon cloud.

Drifting, I am companion to the gulls;
Resting, I remember the herd of deer.
Who knows that this fugitive from the world
Learns at his life’s end to follow the army?

On this day, on the road to the sky’s edge,
In hardship I can only pity myself.
The sea wind stinks and brews rain;
The mountain air is poisonous and holds smoke.

On the dreaded road I follow behind others;
Stumbling through mud I press on before the horse.
Only now do I know the bitterness of marching,
Mostly on the frontier soldier’s side.

Lodging on the road, I grieve at the Cold Food Festival;
Weapons and war have aged the years.
After passing through nine deaths,
My mind is the state before birth.

For the northern campaign I think of Shanfu;
For the southern march I remember Ma Yuan.
From what place comes the plum-blossom flute?
I hear it through and cannot sleep

With one bowl I follow the army;
On a lone march I drift like a tumbleweed.
This bodily frame rides the wild horses of the air;
My heart’s affair is entrusted to the dark goose.

The clouds rise white over Cangwu;
The sunset glow steams red on the sea’s sun.
My life was long ago cast away;
I need not wait for this hour to be empty.

In this floating world I gladly am a guest;
I resent this body that toils through life.
So long as the tongue remains, there is suffering;
Since the Way is here, how can I be called poor?

Thirsty deer race toward the mirage flame;
Hungry crows grow used to coming near men.
Though the seas and fields have not yet changed,
What place is there where dust does not fly?

One breath is the surplus of this remnant life;
The road away through a thousand mountains is long.
Asking the way, I meet a horseherd;
Clutching a book, I pair with the man who lost his sheep.

My heart burns as in the three autumn days;
My mind is cold as the sixth-month frost.
Wherever I pass is like treading a cauldron;
How dare I give free rein to wildness?

My phantom traces have never dwelt anywhere;
Meeting a mountain, I should go home at once.
Because I see the road ahead grow narrow,
I see this life as slight.

Often I bear the hunger of the man beneath the mulberry;
Always I cherish the starving washerwoman’s need.
What I rejoice in is that there is no twelfth month here,
So I do not look for winter clothes to be sent.

Mingling with the world, I hope to forget its forms;
Facing the troops, I think of herding rams.
Before me I drive the grass of patience;
Baring one shoulder, I twist my robe askew.

The setting sun makes the river seem drunk;
The homeward clouds confuse the color of the trees.
My going and staying are like a weary bird’s,
Lowering little by little toward men.

Leopards and tigers fill the central plain;
The star-bearer’s coach races day and night.
The decree holds no word of grief or pity;
Men sit with their faces to the wall in sorrow.

Far off they probe the black dragon’s lair;
Deep they split the weak tree’s branch.
Heaven and earth — for a while I bow and lift my head;
My grief is utter in these two brows.

At Qinghai a victory was just won;
At Zhuyai the campaign was first called off.
Red-feathered dispatches fly through Sword Gate;
Qiang soldiers run along the plank roads.

The emperor’s ear favors the winning of distant peoples;
The royal army is ashamed of war.
The barbarians should be bound with long ropes —
Yet no one asks for the long cord.

Yellow dust darkens all that meets the eye;
My shorn hair hangs down over my shoulders.
The homeward road through rivers and lakes is faint;
Gulls and herons eye a man with doubt.

Of relief and succor I think today;
For safety and peril I look to this hour.
From of old the sable and jade ear-ornaments carry weight —
How can one not be ashamed before such grace?

Ten thousand ravines come rushing down;
A thousand peaks join purple and green.
The sail flies through the Three Gorges rain;
A man enters the ninth-autumn sky.

The traveler’s road lies beyond the floating clouds;
The homeward heart rests before the setting sun.
This life of mine is not yet over;
The rivers and Han are my remaining years.
